#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Score a skill's behaviour evals (``evals.json``) and report ship/no-ship.

Opt-in per skill: a skill is scored only if it ships ``evals/score-evals.json``.
Without that file the skill is skipped cleanly, so this tool stays runnable
across every skill rather than erroring on the ones that never opted in. Once
the config exists, a file it declares but that is missing is a hard error.

What it does, in one pass:

1. Materializes two arms into a scratch workspace — ``new/`` from the working
   tree and ``base/`` from a git ref — expanding the config's ``skill_paths``
   prefixes against each arm separately, because the two arms genuinely carry
   different ``references/`` filenames and counts.
2. Dispatches one blind runner per (chunk x arm) through a coding-agent CLI.
   The rule text is inlined into the prompt, the working directory is empty,
   and the cases handed over carry ``id`` and ``prompt`` only — nothing the
   harness sends a runner names an expectation or points at ``evals/``.
   How firm that is differs by family: ``claude`` runs with ``--tools=`` and
   can read nothing at all, while ``codex`` runs under ``-s read-only``, which
   restricts writes and not reads — that runner keeps filesystem access, and
   the inlined SKILL.md frontmatter names the skill, so a determined agent
   could go looking for the fixture. The blinding is in what is supplied, not
   in a sandbox, on the codex side.
3. Dispatches one blind grader per chunk on the other CLI family, with both
   arms' outputs labelled A/B under a per-run mapping, that chunk's scoring
   key, the source prompts the outputs came from, and the protocol's criteria
   section verbatim. The grader never sees the skill, the version labels, or
   any previous results file.
4. Reconciles and reports. The verdict class of every row is decided here,
   not by the grader, so the "zero protection-class false kills" gate means
   the same thing in every chunk and every run.

Everything runs through coding-agent CLIs (``codex exec`` / ``claude -p``);
this repo never spends a billed vendor API key on eval dispatch.
"""

import sys
from pathlib import Path

# tools/ is not a package root, so put it on sys.path before importing score_evals.
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))

from score_evals.cli import main

if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
